 <div class="note">
-<strong>Example: Tirisfal.</strong> The place name <em>Tirisfal</em> (“Tyr’s Fall”) is a
+<strong>Example: Tirisfal.</strong> The place name <em>Tirisfal</em> ("Tyr's Fall") is a
 Vrykul word that became a Common place name. This is direct evidence of Vrykul &rarr; Common
 borrowing. The Vrykul compound <em>Tiris</em> (Tyr) + <em>-fal</em> (fall) was adopted
 into Common as a single unanalyzed place name, demonstrating that Common speakers no longer
 parse the Vrykul morpheme boundaries — the compound has been lexicalized.
+
+<p><strong>Additional evidence: Nethergarde.</strong> The place name <em>Nethergarde</em>
+provides a second attested example of the Vrykul <em>-garde</em> suffix surviving in Common.
+Unlike <em>Stromgarde</em> (which pairs a potentially Vrykul root <em>Strom</em> with
+<em>-garde</em>), <em>Nethergarde</em> pairs a transparent Common root <em>Nether</em>
+("lower/under") with <em>-garde</em>, demonstrating that the Vrykul suffix was productively
+combined with Common vocabulary — it was not merely fossilized in a single borrowed word.</p>
+
+<p><strong>Personal name evidence.</strong> Several human personal names may preserve Vrykul
+morpheme elements: <em>Thoradin</em> (Vrykul <em>Thor-</em> + <em>-din</em> ~ <em>-inn</em>),
+<em>Uther</em> (Vrykul /θ/ phoneme rare in Common), <em>Tirion</em> (possibly related to
+<em>Tiris-</em> = Tyr), and <em>Taelia</em> (possibly related to Titan <em>Tael-</em> in
+weapon names). These are uncertain but consistent with the hypothesis that Common retains
+scattered Vrykul morphological elements in personal names.</p>
 </div>
 
