+<h3>Investigation: Drust *-kor* / *-kron* and Vrykul *-korn*</h3>
+
+<div class="note">
+<strong>Question:</strong> Is the *-kor* / *-kron* element in the Drust name <em>Lorkron</em>
+(the Hunter) related to the Vrykul clan suffix <em>-korn</em> (as in <em>Winterskorn</em>,
+<em>Tideskorn</em>, <em>Felskorn</em>)?
+
+<h4>Arguments for a connection:</h4>
+<ul>
+<li><strong>Phonological match:</strong> Drust *-kron* /kɾɔn/ is very close to Vrykul *-korn* /kɔɾn/.
+The difference (metathesis of /ɾ/ and /n/, or simple loss of final /n/) is consistent with
+the general pattern of Drust phonological simplification from Vrykul.</li>
+<li><strong>Genetic relationship:</strong> Drust is a confirmed daughter of Vrykul. Inheriting
+a Vrykul suffix is entirely plausible.</li>
+<li><strong>Structural position:</strong> Both *-korn* and *-kron* appear as the second element
+in right-headed compounds, consistent with the inherited compounding pattern.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h4>Arguments against:</h4>
+<ul>
+<li><strong>Single data point:</strong> *-kron* appears in only one Drust name, while *-korn*
+appears in three Vrykul clan names.</li>
+<li><strong>Functional difference:</strong> Vrykul *-korn* is a <strong>clan suffix</strong>
+(group names), while *-kron* appears in a <strong>personal name</strong>. This would require
+a semantic shift from "kin/group" to a general compound-forming element.</li>
+<li><strong>Alternative etymologies:</strong> *Lorkron* could contain Titan *-ron* (honorific)
+or be unanalyzed.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h4>Conclusion:</h4>
+<p>The phonological match is good and the genetic relationship supports inheritance, but the
+single data point and functional difference prevent a confident conclusion. If the connection
+is real, the most likely scenario is: Vrykul *-korn* (clan suffix, "kin/seed") &rarr; Drust
+*-kron* (personal name element) with regular loss of final /n/ or metathesis, and semantic
+shift from "kin-group" to "personal name compound element" as Drust society shifted from
+clan-based to individual-based identity. <strong>Confidence: Low&ndash;Medium.</strong></p>
+</div>
+
