+<h2 id="crossref">Cross-Reference: Apostrophe-Marked Compounds Across "Alien" Languages</h2>
+
+<p>Four WoW languages share apostrophe-marked morpheme boundaries. This section analyzes
+whether the convention reflects genuine linguistic structure, Blizzard design aesthetics,
+or in-universe areal influence.</p>
+
+<h3>Apostrophe Patterns</h3>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Language</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Examples</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
+<tr><td>Shath'Yar</td><td>Initial C' (prefix boundary)</td><td>*N'Zoth*, *C'Thun*, *K'thir*</td><td>Very high</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Nerubian</td><td>Medial CVC' (compound boundary)</td><td>*Anub'azal*, *Ovi'nax*, *Krix'vizk*</td><td>Very high</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Qiraji</td><td>Medial CVC' (compound boundary)</td><td>*Ahn'qiraj*</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Ethereal</td><td>Both initial and medial</td><td>*K'aresh*, *Ve'nari*</td><td>Low</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<div class="note">
+<strong>Key finding:</strong> Shath'Yar uses apostrophes to mark <em>prefix</em> boundaries
+(C' + root), while nerubian/qiraji/ethereal use them for <em>compound</em> boundaries
+(root + root). This structural difference suggests the convention is not a single shared
+feature but may have different origins in each language family.
+<br><br>
+<strong>Old God transmission chain (plausible):</strong> Shath'Yar &rarr; Aqir (Old God
+creation) &rarr; Nerubian + Qiraji (Aqir descendants). The apostrophe convention shifted
+from prefix-marking to compound-marking during Aqir development.
+<br><br>
+<strong>Ethereal position:</strong> Ethereals are NOT Old God creations (native to K'aresh).
+Their apostrophe usage is most likely either independent development or Blizzard aesthetic
+choice, not evidence of in-universe linguistic contact with Aqir-descended species.
+<br><br>
+<strong>Confidence:</strong> Low&ndash;Moderate for the Aqir-inheritance chain;
+High for Blizzard design aesthetic as at least a contributing factor.
+</div>
+
