+<tr><td>*On'hiea*</td><td class="ipa">/ɒnˈhiːə/</td><td>*-iea*</td><td>/iː/ (high front)</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>The surface variation may reflect a single underlying suffix *-ia* /jə/ with phonological conditioning, or the common element may be final *-a* (a feminine suffix) with the preceding vowel belonging to the stem. No male Haranir names end in *-a*. The *-a* feminine ending is a widespread Blizzard design pattern across WoW languages (Draenei, Nazja), making it difficult to distinguish genuine morphology from naming aesthetics.</p>
+
+<p><strong>Confidence: Very Low–Low.</strong></p>
+
+<h3>Haranir–Nerubian Geographic Proximity</h3>
+
+<p>Wildcamp Ul'ar is located near the City of Threads in Azj-Kahet, placing Haranir and nerubians in direct geographic proximity. The *-ar* element in <span class="inferred">*Ul'ar*</span> parallels the nerubian <span class="inferred">*-ar*</span> faction suffix (Anub'ar, Nerub'ar, Hath'ar). If *-ar* were a nerubian loanword, the semantic shift from "brood/collective" to "camp/settlement" would be natural. However, *-ar* is a common syllable, and no other Haranir–nerubian shared morphemes exist.</p>
+
+<p><strong>Confidence: Very Low</strong> for *-ar* as a nerubian loanword.</p>
+
+<h3>Greenspeaker / Thornspeaker Parallel</h3>
+
+<p>The Haranir title <strong>Greenspeaker</strong> (Na'layro) parallels the Drust title <strong>Thornspeaker</strong> (Ulfar et al.). Both follow the English compound pattern [Nature-element] + [speaker]. The parallel is most likely due to independent development: both cultures have druidic traditions, and the naming pattern is a natural English compound. Geographic and temporal separation (Drust in Kul Tiras ~2,600 years ago vs. Haranir in Harandar since ~16,000 BDP) makes contact borrowing extremely unlikely.</p>
+
+<p><strong>Confidence: Very Low</strong> for genuine in-universe connection; <strong>High</strong> for independent parallel development.</p>
+
