 <p>The Haranir title <strong>Greenspeaker</strong> (Na'layro) parallels the Drust title <strong>Thornspeaker</strong> (Ulfar et al.). Both follow the English compound pattern [Nature-element] + [speaker]. The parallel is most likely due to independent development: both cultures have druidic traditions, and the naming pattern is a natural English compound. Geographic and temporal separation (Drust in Kul Tiras ~2,600 years ago vs. Haranir in Harandar since ~16,000 BDP) makes contact borrowing extremely unlikely.</p>
 
 <p><strong>Confidence: Very Low</strong> for genuine in-universe connection; <strong>High</strong> for independent parallel development.</p>
 
+<h3 id="xhosa-comparison">Xhosa Phonological Comparison: Hara'ni vs. Zandali</h3>
+
+<p>The confirmed shared Xhosa accent (warcraft.wiki.gg; Amarakk voiced by Ike Amadi, who also voices Rokhan) implies that both languages inherited phonological features from proto-troll. Detailed analysis in <a href="grammar.html">grammar.md</a>.</p>
+
+<h4>Shared Features (Potential Proto-Troll Substrate)</h4>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Hara'ni</th><th>Zandali</th><th>Xhosa Parallel</th></tr>
+<tr><td>Vowel system</td><td>/a e i o u/</td><td>/a e i o u/</td><td>Identical five-vowel system</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Syllable structure</td><td>(C)V(C) preferred</td><td>(C)V(C) preferred</td><td>Xhosa favors open (C)V syllables</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Apostrophe compounding</td><td>Very high (~25/30 names)</td><td>High (~20/40 names)</td><td>Morpheme boundaries phonologically salient</td></tr>
+<tr><td>/a/ and /i/ frequency</td><td>Highest</td><td>Highest</td><td>Consistent with Xhosa distribution</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Palato-alveolar /ʃ/ /tʃ/</td><td>Present</td><td>Present</td><td>Xhosa has this series</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Medial /r/</td><td>Common</td><td>Common</td><td>Xhosa allows /r/ medially</td></tr>
+<tr><td>No complex clusters</td><td>Mostly simple</td><td>Mostly simple</td><td>Xhosa strongly avoids clusters</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h4>Divergent Features (Post-Divergence Innovations)</h4>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Hara'ni</th><th>Zandali</th><th>Analysis</th></tr>
+<tr><td>/h/ frequency</td><td>Very high</td><td>Low–Moderate</td><td>Hara'ni innovation in isolation</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Geminates / long vowels</td><td>Present (-nn-, -kk-, -uu-)</td><td>Absent</td><td>Hara'ni innovation</td></tr>
+<tr><td>/θ/, /ʒ/</td><td>Present</td><td>Absent</td><td>Hara'ni innovation</td></tr>
+<tr><td>/f/, /v/, /z/</td><td>Rare</td><td>Common</td><td>Zandali retention; Hara'ni loss</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Pre-nasalized stops</td><td>Absent</td><td>Present (Bwonsamdi)</td><td>Zandali retention of Xhosa-like feature?</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h4>Assessment</h4>
+<p><strong>Overall Confidence: Moderate</strong> that the shared Xhosa accent reflects genuine proto-troll phonological features, given: (1) confirmed genetic relationship, (2) multiple shared features consistent with Xhosa-like substrate, (3) same voice actor linking both races, (4) systematic differences explicable as post-divergence innovations.</p>
